Title: Cap the chunker below the embedder's token limit before seg/1 ships
Created: 2026-08-29
Author: claude
Label: needs-human

--- log ---
2026-08-29T15:00Z claude created
2026-08-29T15:00Z claude label needs-human

--- body ---
## Context
DESIGN.md §2.2 chunks at 120 / 768 / 48 tokens, "Zotero's geometry, adopted
verbatim". The embedder cannot read a chunk that large, and it does not say
so when it fails.

Measured 2026-08-29, reproducing zoteus's exact call
(`extractor(batch, {pooling:'mean', normalize:true})`, no truncation flag)
against the model it loads, `Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2`:

- The model's limit is 512 tokens (`max_position_embeddings: 512`,
  `tokenizer_config.model_max_length: 512`), read from the cached model
  itself. This is BERT's learned absolute position table, fixed at
  pretraining in 2018 and inherited by every distillate since. It was not
  chosen for retrieval.
- A 1 862-token input (902 tokens on one topic, 962 on another) returns a
  vector with **cosine 1,000000** against its first 512 tokens alone. No
  error, no warning. Positive control: the two topics score 0,293747
  against each other, so the 1,0 is a real identity and not an artifact of
  similarity.

Probe: `verification/probes/embedder-truncation-probe.mjs`.

## Why this is prospective, not a live bug
Zoteus today is safe, and safe by accident. Its chunker caps in
**characters**, not tokens: `FULLTEXT_CHUNK_SIZE = 1200` and
`META_CHUNK_SIZE = 512` (index-manager.ts), which land near 300 and 128
tokens. Nothing is truncated in the field today, and an earlier reading of
this evidence that called it a live upstream defect was wrong.

The exposure arrives with our own redesign. 768 tokens is roughly 3 000
characters, about 2,5x zoteus's current body chunk, and the first geometry
in this pipeline that exceeds what the embedder can read. **The migration to
token-structural chunking removes an accidental safety property**, which is
why this must be settled before seg/1 is built rather than after.

## Why it matters on this corpus
Paragraph-size distribution over 1 500 random `.zotero-ft-cache` files of
13 631 (`verification/probes/paragraph-size-distribution.py`), split by
attachment type because a third of the store is HTML snapshots whose
extraction is navigation chrome one word per line:

| block size | share of PDF word mass |
|---|---|
| 150–350 w | 23,3 % |
| 350–600 w | 40,0 % |
| 600+ w | 20,6 % |

83,9 % of PDF text sits in blocks of 150 words or more, 60,6 % in blocks of
350+ (roughly 470+ tokens, a derived figure at ~1,35 tokens per word — a
real tokenizer would sharpen it). Those are precisely the blocks that cross
512 under a 768-token cap. The failure would be silent partial embedding:
searchable in full lexically, represented only to the first 512 tokens
semantically, with nothing in the index, the logs or the coverage report
saying so. That is an R4 and R17 violation invisible to any test that does
not compare two vectors.

## The architectural constraint on the fix
Do not derive the chunker's maximum from the embedder's limit. Under C1,
chunks derive from text plus **chunker** identity and geometry, and vectors
derive from chunks plus **embedder** identity and model. If chunk geometry
is a function of the model's limit, the chunk key depends on the embedder,
so swapping models re-derives chunks, FTS rows and slabs rather than only
vectors. That collapses a stage boundary C1 exists to keep clean, and the
cost only appears the day the model changes.

## Actions
1. Choose a fixed body budget plus heading-prefix budget summing below 512
   tokens, stated as a constant. Record the ruling in DECISIONS.md before
   DESIGN.md moves; 768 was ratified as "adopted verbatim" and replacing it
   is a change to a ratified reading.
2. State in §2.2 what binds and why, including that Zotero's own effective
   ceiling is not 768 either — so this is a divergence from a number, not
   from a geometry.
3. Recompute §2.9's ≈ 250–300k passage estimate at the new cap. **Measure,
   do not divide.** The max rarely binds under structural chunking, since an
   entry shorter than the ceiling yields one chunk regardless, so arithmetic
   on the ratio would overstate the correction.
4. Assert the cap in code, and add `truncation: true` to the embed call so a
   future over-length chunk fails loudly instead of silently. Consider the
   flag as a small contained upstream PR on its own merits; it fixes a
   latent risk rather than a live bug, so it competes for budget against
   items that fix real ones.
5. Evaluate a long-context embedder (BGE-M3, Nomic, Jina v2 — all ~8 192
   tokens, all local via ONNX) as insurance rather than as the fix. BGE-M3
   would also serve C2's CJK path. Note the standing caveat: a larger window
   removes the truncation defect and does not make large chunks good, since
   one vector is a fixed-size summary and averaging more text into it
   degrades retrieval whatever the model accepts.

## Test
A regression test that embeds a text whose head exceeds the cap and whose
tail is semantically unrelated, then asserts the vector is NOT identical to
the head's. Two constructions of this test give false answers and both were
hit while producing the evidence above: a repeated single word makes
mean-pooling length-invariant, so the test passes vacuously; and a head
shorter than the limit means the two texts never share a full-length prefix.
The test must assert its own positive control — head and tail dissimilar —
or it cannot distinguish truncation from similarity.

## Verification
- [ ] The cap is a stated constant, not derived from the model
- [ ] The regression test fails against a 768-token cap and passes at the new one
- [ ] §2.9's passage count is measured at the new cap, not divided
- [ ] `make check` green, figure-guard anchors updated

## Invariants
- C1's stage separation holds: the chunk key does not depend on the embedder.
- The entry as unit of answer (ratified 2026-08-26) is unchanged; this
  bounds chunk size within an entry, it does not redefine the entry.
- Coverage honesty (R4, R17) is the point of the ticket, not a side effect.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] The cap is ratified in DECISIONS.md and stated in DESIGN.md §2.2
- [ ] §2.9 recomputed from a measurement
- [ ] The regression test exists, with its positive control
- [ ] Blocks 0028: seg/1 is built to the settled geometry, never to 768
